# What never needs to reach the Docker daemon.
#
# Every image here builds from the repository root, because the Rust build needs `/VERSION` and the
# whole `rust/` workspace. Without this file that context is several gigabytes of build output and
# git history, which is slow locally and slower in CI — and the cargo `target/` in particular would be
# copied in and then shadowed by the container's own target directory, so it is pure waste.

# Build output
target/
build/
library/java/*/build/
library/java/*/bin/
library/java/build-logic/build/
.gradle/
node_modules/
app/ui/dist/
app/ui/node_modules/
app/gen/

# Version control and tooling
.git/
.github/
.idea/
.vscode/

# Runtime artifacts (see .gitignore for why these exist)
/run/
/nodera/
/logs/
/world-store/
/divergences/
.jqwik-database

# Everything the services do not build from
docs/
fixtures/
scripts/
*.md
*.jpg
*.png

# --- what docker/web/Dockerfile needs back ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The three rules above are right for a Rust service and wrong for a website: a site is exactly the
# markdown, the screenshots and the build script the services have no use for. Docker takes the LAST
# matching pattern, so these are narrow re-inclusions rather than deletions — docs/, fixtures/ and
# the rest of scripts/ stay out of every build context, including this one.
#
# Losing one of these is silent. The site simply builds without the file, and the image serves a
# page with a missing image or 404s a route nobody tested.
!web/**
!scripts/build-site.sh
!scripts/lib/layout.sh

# …and back out again, after the re-inclusion, because `!web/**` would otherwise drag in whatever
# the site's package manager left lying around. `node_modules/` above is anchored at the context
# root and never matched web/node_modules. web/dist is a build output: the image produces its own
# inside the builder, and copying a laptop's in would only shadow it for one layer.
web/node_modules/
web/dist/
